From 5feab0ad405cb04de7fab9bceeca519aa768eb98 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 15:59:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 67/81] vulkan/wsi: Horizon, one dequeue policy at every
 timeout
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 4 (Horizon WSI backend)
Why: the acquire had two dequeue modes and a timeout-dependent rule for
 choosing between them. The second, async=false, is the mode Android
 permits the server to block in, and nothing in this file can bound how
 long: neither the deadline check at the top of the loop nor 0064's
 sleep can run until the call returns. 0065 narrowed it to
 timeout==UINT64_MAX so it could not overrun a caller's deadline, which
 also made it unreachable from every test in this tree.
Evidence: hardware run 13 (2026-08-08, build 2026-08-08T15:40:28.620Z
 b958bd0 mesa:587ac72), the first run in which no acquire could reach
 async=false. t_vk_swapchain PASS 120/120: "2 images, FIFO: 90 of 90
 frames presented", "89 intervals, mean 16551 us ... 87 within 10% of
 16666 us", "acquire mean 15719 us / max 21303 us". t_nwindow PASS
 118/118 through raw bq* with the same policy: "2 buffers, interval 1:
 90 of 90 frames presented", "87 within 10% of 16666 us", "dequeue mean
 2254 us / max 6581 us". async=true plus a real sleep is sufficient on
 its own, on the two-buffer window that motivated the second mode.
Upstream: no — this file is the Horizon backend itself.

The 2026-08-05 measurement that put async=false here — async=true
answering NO_INIT 78166 times running while async=false handed over a
buffer in 145 us — was taken from a loop with no idle in it, spinning
bqDequeueBuffer at roughly 17000 binder transactions a second into the
compositor it was waiting for. It measured the spin, not the mode.
0064 removed the spin; run 13 removes the reason for the mode.

What this leaves is one policy at every timeout, so the loop the finite
case exercises is the loop the infinite case runs and the only
difference is which deadline check fires. The `blocking` parameter goes
with it: it had one caller and, after 0065, one value.

This also settles the naming finding from the PR #8 review — `blocking`
was the negation of the `async` argument it controlled — by deleting
the parameter rather than renaming it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c | 70 ++++++++++++++++++------------------
 1 file changed, 35 insertions(+), 35 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
index 5f8ae52..d62de7c 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
@@ -984,26 +984,27 @@ wsi_horizon_dequeue_would_block(Result rc)
                         LibnxBinderError_InvalidOperation);
 }
 
-/* One dequeue mode: libnx's.
+/* One dequeue mode, and now only one: async=true, libnx's own.
  *
  * libnx passes the producer's `async` flag as true on any window that
  * has a release event and false only on one that does not
  * (nx/source/display/native_window.c, nwindowDequeueBuffer). The
- * default window has one, so async=true is the platform's path and
- * async=false is a mode nothing here is known to use. `blocking` is
- * kept as a parameter so a caller can still ask in the other mode
- * deliberately; the acquire does not.
+ * default window has one, so async=true is the platform's path.
  *
  * WHAT THIS COMMENT CLAIMED BEFORE, AND WHAT REFUTED IT. It said async
  * mode asks the queue to keep a buffer in reserve, which a two-buffer
  * queue cannot do, and that switching to async=false after a release
  * was what made two images work. Measured on 2026-08-05 with two
  * buffers queued and the producer holding none: an async=true dequeue
- * succeeded in 104 us. The mode was never the problem — see the
- * acquire loop below for what was.
+ * succeeded in 104 us. The mode was never the problem — the acquire
+ * loop's missing idle was.
+ *
+ * The `blocking` parameter went with the second mode. It existed only
+ * for the acquire's fallback attempt, and a parameter with one caller
+ * and one value is a way to write the constant twice.
  */
 static Result
-wsi_horizon_try_dequeue(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain, bool blocking,
+wsi_horizon_try_dequeue(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
                         s32 *slot_out, NvMultiFence *fence_out)
 {
    NWindow *win = chain->window;
@@ -1011,7 +1012,8 @@ wsi_horizon_try_dequeue(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain, bool blocking,
    NvMultiFence fence;
    memset(&fence, 0, sizeof(fence));
 
-   Result rc = bqDequeueBuffer(&win->bq, !blocking, win->width, win->height,
+   Result rc = bqDequeueBuffer(&win->bq, true /* async, as libnx does */,
+                               win->width, win->height,
                                (s32)win->format, win->usage, &slot, &fence);
    if (R_FAILED(rc))
       return rc;
@@ -1122,37 +1124,35 @@ wsi_horizon_acquire_zero_copy(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
    for (;;) {
       s32 slot = -1;
       NvMultiFence fence;
-      Result rc = wsi_horizon_try_dequeue(chain, false /* libnx's mode */,
-                                          &slot, &fence);
+      Result rc = wsi_horizon_try_dequeue(chain, &slot, &fence);
 
-      /* THE SECOND MODE, AND ONLY WHERE IT CANNOT OVERRUN THE CALLER.
+      /* THE SECOND MODE IS GONE, AND THIS IS WHAT RETIRED IT.
+       *
+       * It was asked when async=true said "no buffer" and the caller
+       * had given no deadline to overrun: async=false, the mode Android
+       * permits to block in the server. 0065 narrowed it to that case
+       * because nothing here can bound how long it blocks — neither the
+       * deadline check at the top of this loop nor 0064's sleep can run
+       * until the call returns.
        *
-       * Measured on hardware (2026-08-05) on a two-buffer window with
-       * both buffers queued: async=true answered NO_INIT 78166 times
-       * running, and async=false handed over a buffer in 145 us without
-       * blocking. Android's producer reads `async` as "this producer is
-       * in asynchronous mode" — queueBuffer never blocks and older
-       * frames are dropped — which costs the queue one buffer held in
-       * reserve. A two-buffer queue with both buffers out cannot spare
-       * it. FIFO is synchronous by definition, so async=false is the
-       * mode that describes what a swapchain is doing.
+       * That narrowing made it unreachable in practice, since every
+       * acquire in both tests carries a finite budget, and run 13
+       * (2026-08-08, b958bd0) measured what was left: two images
+       * present 90 of 90 with 87 of 89 intervals inside 10% of a
+       * refresh, acquire mean 15719 us; two raw buffers through
+       * t_nwindow the same, 90 of 90 and 87 of 89. async=true plus a
+       * real sleep is sufficient on its own.
        *
-       * BUT IT IS THE MODE ANDROID PERMITS TO BLOCK IN THE SERVER, and
-       * nothing here can bound how long. Neither the deadline check at
-       * the top of this loop nor 0064's sleep can run until the call
-       * returns, so on any finite timeout it could overrun what
-       * vkAcquireNextImageKHR promised — and "measured at 134-158 us on
-       * this compositor" is evidence that it does not, not that it
-       * cannot. Reported by Codex on PR #8, and right.
+       * The 2026-08-05 measurement that motivated the second mode —
+       * async=true answering NO_INIT 78166 times running while
+       * async=false handed over a buffer in 145 us — was taken from a
+       * loop with no idle in it, which was starving the compositor it
+       * was waiting for. It measured the spin, not the mode.
        *
-       * So it is asked only when the caller gave no deadline to
-       * overrun. A finite timeout gets async=true and the sleep, which
-       * is what t_nwindow's copy of this loop uses to present 90 of 90
-       * frames on two buffers.
+       * So the policy is one policy at every timeout, and the loop the
+       * finite case exercises is the loop the infinite case runs: the
+       * only difference is which deadline check fires.
        */
-      if (wsi_horizon_dequeue_would_block(rc) && timeout_ns == UINT64_MAX)
-         rc = wsi_horizon_try_dequeue(chain, true /* FIFO's own mode */,
-                                      &slot, &fence);
 
       if (R_SUCCEEDED(rc)) {
          struct wsi_horizon_image *image = &chain->images[slot];
-- 
